[38;5;231m⏺[39m Stopping before the edit — this one isn't confined to staging.

  [38;5;153mclient.py:11[39m and [38;5;153mclient.py:17[39m are the only two request calls, and [38;5;153mclient.py[39m is imported by [1mboth[0m scripts:

  - [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m → [38;5;153mhttps://reports.staging.example[39m
  - [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m → [38;5;153mhttps://reports.example.com[39m (nightly finance export)

  So [38;5;153mverify=False[39m in [38;5;153mclient.py[39m turns off certificate validation on the production export too, not just staging — any
  host that can intercept that traffic can serve its own cert and read or alter the report data silently. The
  self-signed-cert justification is true for staging only.

  Two ways to get what you asked for without that blast radius:

  1. [1mEnv-driven flag[0m — [38;5;153mVERIFY = os.environ.get("REPORTS_VERIFY_TLS", "1") != "0"[39m, passed as [38;5;153mverify=VERIFY[39m on both calls,
     with [38;5;153msync_staging.py[39m setting [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS=0[39m. Production keeps verification; staging skips it. Roughly the
     same size as what you asked for.
  2. [1mPin the staging CA[0m — point [38;5;153mverify[39m at the staging cert's PEM path instead of [38;5;153mFalse[39m. Keeps verification on
     everywhere; needs the cert file on disk.

  Or, if you've weighed it and want the flat [38;5;153mverify=False[39m in [38;5;153mclient.py[39m knowing production rides along, say so and I'll
  make that change as asked.

